#{[{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046660} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186554725} :activity/id #uuid "6dc7ac15-0a44-5d9e-9fc8-92ea8c7c8833" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048308 :topic/id #uuid "93699c29-7fa0-5489-8437-1976b79a70b4" :topic/math-academy-id 2110} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186554673 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259589 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "06c9589f-c628-5f4a-a78a-70eef556a95c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{e}^{6}-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256839} {:answer/id #uuid "7d69fec3-73fc-5815-8bc3-797953319185" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2{e}^{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257889} {:answer/id #uuid "8020ab34-3c45-5159-8e7f-6c5ce658702c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4{e}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257922} {:answer/id #uuid "da2ca4d2-0f0c-5594-9b9e-6e91ba3983b3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{e}^{3}-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258687} {:answer/id #uuid "e253a7c1-631c-5b5e-ba82-916317b443b4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2{e}^{3}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258750}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186257889} :answer-field/id #uuid "33c80ca6-5207-5a6c-a174-ad7e192a275e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259129}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "388c57e6-e07f-5b15-a475-f9e96568702d" :question/math-academy-id "q-124028" :question/problem "Let $f(x,y)=y{x}^{3}.$ Given that $C$ is the path along the curve $y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ for $x\\in [1,{e}^{2}],$ evaluate ${\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\nWe start by parametrizing the curve $C.$ A suitable parametrization is\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨t,\\operatorname{ln}⁡t⟩,\\,t\\in [1,{e}^{2}].\n$$\n\nThen, the position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(1) & =⟨1,0⟩, \\\\ r({e}^{2}) & =⟨{e}^{2},2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path defined by $C$ are $(1,0)$ and $({e}^{2},2).$\n\nApplying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r & =f(r({e}^{2}))-f(r(1)) \\\\  & =f({e}^{2},2)-f(1,0) \\\\  & =2\\cdot {({e}^{2})}^{3}-0\\cdot {1}^{3} \\\\  & =2{e}^{6}-0 \\\\  & =2{e}^{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "483708f5-37ca-59c3-817d-8740001e626d" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186554742}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186554724 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240921 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "30453404-690a-5b85-850f-9aaa09a4efe2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "9{ln}^{3}⁡2-12\\operatorname{ln}⁡2+21" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238475} {:answer/id #uuid "35cdd507-7f75-51ad-acf4-534f3b62ea96" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{ln}^{2}⁡2+9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238522} {:answer/id #uuid "58c144e5-2b4f-5201-b0bb-e144657253c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{ln}^{3}⁡2-63" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238819} {:answer/id #uuid "da6b93e1-2f2d-5dd2-9235-e2c75c2150c5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{ln}^{3}⁡2+\\operatorname{ln}⁡2-9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239960} {:answer/id #uuid "e08284d3-5a39-5136-ad35-3aadf74bdc85" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240022}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186238475} :answer-field/id #uuid "6a6c211e-df9c-57af-8d94-442d4251996d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240527}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "7794dd99-ebef-5626-aacd-8b9fce4aa6b2" :question/math-academy-id "q-104733" :question/problem "Consider the curve $C,$ defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨3\\operatorname{ln}⁡t,{t}^{2}⟩,\\,1\\le t\\le 2.\n$$\n\nEvaluate ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r$ for $F=({x}^{2}-y)\\,i+({y}^{2}-x)\\,j.$ \n\n*Hint: You may wish to use the fact that $F$ is conservative.*" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nTo apply the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we must first find a potential function $f$ such that $F=∇f.$ Since we're given that $F$ is conservative, we know that such a function exists. Therefore, we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & ={x}^{2}-y, \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y) & ={y}^{2}-x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nReconstructing the function $f$ from its gradient in the usual way, we get\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}+\\frac{1}{3}{y}^{3}-xy.\n$$\n\nNote that we set $C=0$ for the arbitrary constant since we can use any function $f$ that satisfies $∇f=F.$\n\nWe're now ready to apply the fundamental theorem for line integrals. The position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(1) & =⟨0,1⟩, \\\\ r(2) & =⟨3\\operatorname{ln}⁡2,4⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path defined by $C$ are $(0,1)$ and $(3\\operatorname{ln}⁡2,4).$\n\nFinally, applying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & =f(3\\operatorname{ln}⁡2,4)-f(0,1) \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot (3\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)^{3}+\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot {4}^{3}-3\\operatorname{ln}⁡2\\cdot 4)-(\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot {0}^{3}+\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot {1}^{3}-0\\cdot 1) \\\\  & =9{ln}^{3}⁡2+\\frac{64}{3}-12\\operatorname{ln}⁡2-\\frac{1}{3} \\\\  & =9{ln}^{3}⁡2-12\\operatorname{ln}⁡2+21.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "b2250ee8-d365-5b15-95dc-e924af190b6e" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186554732}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186554725 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240919 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "741bc569-8dcc-54a7-93f1-c3d9b892adc0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{e}^{{\\pi }^{2}+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239049} {:answer/id #uuid "76597f8f-a721-552f-bc46-44e414558f67" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "e({e}^{4{\\pi }^{2}}-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239077} {:answer/id #uuid "a05094d0-34c2-5de5-a965-8a05daab4caa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{e}^{4\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239454} {:answer/id #uuid "a811fdf6-0ca8-5ba9-9b33-e28fa7b6d1c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239513} {:answer/id #uuid "dbca776b-42f8-568c-8d08-0e204fad75f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "e({e}^{2\\pi }-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239973}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186239077} :answer-field/id #uuid "4c402c1d-d0dd-5148-a3d3-ef8f8ca1b754" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240462}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "30f2f02c-3a13-5617-8d20-05ffc71d40c0" :question/math-academy-id "q-104728" :question/problem "Consider the curve $C,$ defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨{t}^{2}+1,t⟩,\\,0\\le t\\le 2\\pi .\n$$\n\nEvaluate ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r$ for $F={e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\,i-{e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\,j.$ \n\n*Hint: You may wish to use the fact that $F$ is conservative.*" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nTo apply the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we must first find a potential function $f$ such that $F=∇f.$ Since we're given that $F$ is conservative, we know that such a function exists. Therefore, we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & ={e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y, \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y) & =-{e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nReconstructing the function $f$ from its gradient in the usual way, we get\n\n$$\nf(x,y)={e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y.\n$$\n\nNote that we set $C=0$ for the arbitrary constant since we can use any function $f$ that satisfies $∇f=F.$\n\nWe're now ready to apply the fundamental theorem for line integrals. The position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨1,0⟩, \\\\ r(2\\pi ) & =⟨4{\\pi }^{2}+1,2\\pi ⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path defined by $C$ are $(1,0)$ and $(4{\\pi }^{2}+1,2\\pi ).$\n\nFinally, applying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & =f(4{\\pi }^{2}+1,2\\pi )-f(1,0) \\\\  & ={e}^{4{\\pi }^{2}+1}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\pi -{e}^{1}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡0 \\\\  & ={e}^{4{\\pi }^{2}+1}-e \\\\  & =e({e}^{4{\\pi }^{2}}-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "b60329da-a444-57a9-b50b-19ebe65ffae5" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186554724}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186554732 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240926 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "03d4147a-c71a-5c94-85e5-f5d9a8038924" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(e-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238114} {:answer/id #uuid "27bb17e7-e9f0-57ab-a950-9c30d0a83e5a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡e-\\operatorname{sin}⁡2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238398} {:answer/id #uuid "5d7a11a7-e4b9-5d99-a4e6-6b05e88774ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238870} {:answer/id #uuid "6d475b88-50f1-508c-94ec-f40b5b005181" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{sin}⁡e-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238994} {:answer/id #uuid "e3f3259a-1dbd-5b91-9075-78bd6e17f1dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡2e-\\operatorname{sin}⁡{e}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240058}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186238870} :answer-field/id #uuid "7afbb94e-3c3c-582d-befe-a39fc2c5fb10" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240551}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "b5c03f65-0c80-5089-93df-00e7a5829280" :question/math-academy-id "q-104747" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F,$ defined as\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,j.\n$$\n\nEvaluate ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r,$ where $C$ is the curve $y={e}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ for $x\\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}].$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\nFirst, we define $P(x,y)$ and $Q(x,y)$ as follows:\n\n$$\nF(x,y)={{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y}_{⏟}}_{P(x,y)}\\,i+{{\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}_{⏟}}_{Q(x,y)}\\,j.\n$$\n\nTo apply the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we must first find a potential function $f$ (if it exists) such that $F=∇f.$ Notice that the domain of $F$ is ${R}^{2},$ and\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y.\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives are continuous and equal everywhere on ${R}^{2},$ there exists a potential function $f$ such that $F=∇f,$ and we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y, \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nReconstructing the function $f$ from its gradient in the usual way, we get\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y.\n$$\n\nNote that we set $C=0$ for the arbitrary constant since we can use any function $f$ that satisfies $∇f=F.$\n\nWe're now ready to apply the fundamental theorem for line integrals. A suitable parametrization of the curve $C$ is given by\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨t,{e}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩,\\,t\\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}].\n$$\n\nThe position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨0,2⟩, \\\\ r(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =⟨\\frac{\\pi }{2},e⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path defined by $C$ are $(0,0)$ and $(\\frac{\\pi }{2},e).$\n\nFinally, applying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & =f(\\frac{\\pi }{2},e)-f(0,2) \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡e)-(\\operatorname{sin}⁡0\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡2) \\\\  & =1\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡e-0\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡2 \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡e.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "c701ba9d-2e0f-5001-9a2a-090a2ae8ed57" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186554673}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186554742 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259593 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "228757f4-13cc-55fe-9095-a48e100ceb16" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1-\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257067} {:answer/id #uuid "2a73a14f-a5c6-5495-a6bd-6839c844573f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1+\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257158} {:answer/id #uuid "75872a6e-2056-5f4d-a136-119cb8679c55" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257812} {:answer/id #uuid "c2078454-b9ed-517e-8d5c-46c93d61beb4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258484} {:answer/id #uuid "fdbba96b-984e-5d92-80e9-dd02e8dff0a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259001}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259001} :answer-field/id #uuid "db56c118-70b4-55be-8655-9ad9749d2c6d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259417}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "cc34530a-2e34-53d7-8fcc-b9ff1814815f" :question/math-academy-id "q-124034" :question/problem "Let $f(x,y)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y.$ Given that $C$ is the path along the curve $2x=\\pi {y}^{3}$ for $y\\in [0,1],$ evaluate ${\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\nWe start by parametrizing the curve $C.$ A suitable parametrization is\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨\\frac{\\pi {t}^{3}}{2},t⟩,\\,t\\in [0,1].\n$$\n\nThen, the position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨0,0⟩, \\\\ r(1) & =⟨\\frac{\\pi }{2},1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r & =f(r(1))-f(r(0)) \\\\  & =f(\\frac{\\pi }{2},1)-f(0,0) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\operatorname{cos}⁡1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡0\\operatorname{cos}⁡0 \\\\  & =0\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡1-1\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "e502ee71-f712-5183-adfd-f99482651c2f"}] :activity/title "The Fundamental Theorem for Line Integrals" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553848}]}
